--- (4) CANDIDATE LAWS (weighted LS in linear Phi; weights 1/SE^2) ---
    fit points: SPARC bins (non-empty, SE>0) + O01 primary + O01 deep tail, N = 14
    (a) Phi = c x^p :            c = 2.024 +- 0.101,  p = +0.170 +- 0.026  (z_p = +6.57),  chi2 = 115.4/12 = 9.62
    (b) Phi = 1 + alpha ln x :   c0 = 2.001 +- 0.092,  alpha = +0.273 +- 0.043  (z_alpha = +6.37),  chi2 = 114.6/12 = 9.55
    (c) Phi = (1+x)/(1+x/b) :    b = 52.519 +- 131.074  (family implies Phi(1) = 1.963; the constraint Phi(1)=1 forces b=1, the constant Phi=1, degenerate), chi2 = 181.4/13 = 13.95
    (c2) Phi = c_b(1+x)/(1+x/b), Phi(1)=1:  b = 0.501 +- 0.030  (deep Phi(0) = 1.498, Phi(inf) = 0.751),  chi2 = 223.9/13 = 17.22

--- (5) VERDICT (pre-registered rule) ---
    C1: 33.3% of 9 overlapping comparisons within 3 combined SE (need >= 2/3): FAIL
    C2: power-law exponent p/SE_p = +6.57 (need |z| > 5): PASS
    VERDICT: The sign-mirror is a GENUINE SYSTEMATICS DIVIDE (registered finding): Phi(x) does NOT collapse across surveys at fixed x -- the same conclusion N05's arbitration program would reach, now data-side.
    BEST FIT: form (b);  Phi(x) = 1 +0.273 ln x  [alpha = +0.273 +- 0.043, chi2/dof = 9.55; fit on SPARC bins + O01 dwarfs].  Falsifier: any survey with >= 3 rings in one x-bin whose mean Phi departs > 3 combined SE from this curve; or a flat fit alpha with |alpha|/SE_alpha < 5 on the pooled SPARC+dwarf dataset.

